(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-vignettes"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with symptomatic varicose veins has worn stockings and exercised faithfully for several months with persistent aching. Duplex ultrasound confirms great saphenous vein reflux. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Surgical valvuloplasty","Venous ablation therapy","Lifelong observation only","Oral flavonoid therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A trial of conservative therapy with stockings is generally done for weeks to months before venous ablation therapy is offered. She has completed that trial with persistent aching and duplex confirms great saphenous reflux, so ablation, whose goal is better symptoms and appearance, comes next. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41, 43"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man starts outpatient treatment for deep vein thrombosis with low molecular weight heparin injections overlapped with warfarin. The injections continue for at least five days and until his international normalized ratio is above which value?","choices":["Above 1","Above 2","Above 3","Above 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For outpatient treatment, low molecular weight heparin (enoxaparin) or fondaparinux is given for at least 5 days, overlapping with warfarin, until the international normalized ratio (INR) is above 2. Inpatient unfractionated heparin uses the same cutoff, overlapping warfarin until the INR has been above 2 for at least 24 hours. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a catheter-related deep vein thrombosis of the right arm and asks how likely a pulmonary embolism is compared with a leg clot. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Up to 6%, versus 15 to 30% for leg clots","15 to 30%, versus up to 6% for leg clots","About 3% for arm and leg clots alike","Under 1% for arm clots, 6% for leg clots"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary embolism occurs in up to 6% of upper-extremity deep vein thromboses (DVTs), versus 15% to 30% of lower-extremity ones, so her catheter-related arm clot carries the lower risk. Arm clots are only 5% to 10% of all DVTs and are most often caused by pacemakers, defibrillators or central venous catheters. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with active pancreatic cancer, which supplies the hypercoagulable component of the Virchow triad, has an acute proximal deep vein thrombosis of the left leg. She also has active gastrointestinal bleeding. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Systemic thrombolysis","Outpatient rivaroxaban","Inferior vena cava filter","Unfractionated heparin infusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An inferior vena cava filter is used only when a patient has an acute proximal lower extremity deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and active bleeding, or when anticoagulation is otherwise contraindicated. She has both an acute proximal clot and active gastrointestinal bleeding. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man developed a single large arteriovenous fistula of the forearm after a penetrating injury. It causes swelling and a bruit over the arm. Which is the management for this acquired fistula?","choices":["Surgical removal or size reduction","Compression stockings alone","Warm compresses and elevation","Lifelong anticoagulation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His fistula is acquired, from a penetrating injury, and is a single large connection. Acquired arteriovenous (AV) fistulas are managed surgically, by removing the fistula or decreasing its size; elastic support hose and embolization are the approaches described for congenital fistulas. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with symptomatic varicose veins has a duplex ultrasound showing significant saphenous vein reflux. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Vascular specialist","Cardiologist","Hematologist","Orthopedic surgeon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Significant saphenous reflux is one of the reasons to refer to a vascular specialist, along with arterial insufficiency, nonhealing or recurrent ulcers, persistent stasis dermatitis and diagnostic uncertainty. Varicose veins usually involve the great saphenous vein, so his reflux sits in the vein most often affected. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man receiving an unfractionated heparin infusion for deep vein thrombosis develops life-threatening bleeding. Which agent reverses the heparin?","choices":["Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Andexanet alfa","Vitamin K by mouth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He is on an unfractionated heparin (UFH) infusion and has life-threatening bleeding, the most serious adverse effect of anticoagulation. Protamine sulfate is the reversal agent for UFH and for low molecular weight heparin. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with diabetes has a painful ulcer over the lateral ankle. His foot is cool and the pedal pulses are weak. Which test should be obtained next?","choices":["Punch biopsy of the ulcer","Ankle-brachial index","Venous duplex ultrasound","Catheter-based venography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A painful ulcer usually on the lateral aspect of the ankle with weak pulses fits arterial insufficiency, and he is older with a lower extremity wound. Arterial pulse examination combined with the ankle-brachial index (ABI) should be performed on any such patient. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man develops a first calf deep vein thrombosis one week after minor knee arthroscopy under a local nerve block, followed by a long car trip. Which duration of anticoagulation is appropriate?","choices":["A fixed three months","Indefinitely","Six weeks","Three to six months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Minor surgery and prolonged travel are both minor transient risk factors, and a first venous thromboembolism (VTE) provoked by a minor transient factor is treated for 3 to 6 months. His arthroscopy used a local nerve block, not general anesthesia over 30 minutes, so it is not a major factor. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has symptomatic varicose veins that did not improve with stockings. She prefers a nonsurgical procedure in which a solution is injected to scar and close the veins. Which procedure fits?","choices":["Vein stripping","Radiofrequency ablation","Venous bypass","Sclerotherapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sclerotherapy injects a sclerosing agent to fibrose and obliterate the veins, matching her wish for an injection that scars and closes them. It is offered after a trial of conservative therapy with stockings, which she has had. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 24-year-old soccer player comes to urgent care with left calf pain that began two days ago when he felt a sudden pull while sprinting, and he has bruising around the ankle. He has no leg swelling, tenderness along the deep veins, cancer, cast or history of clots. By the Wells criteria, a calf muscle tear is at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis. Which is the most appropriate first test?","choices":["D-dimer blood test","Venous duplex ultrasound","Contrast ascending venography","Computed tomography of the leg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He has no positive Wells items, and a calf muscle tear at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis (DVT) subtracts 2, so his score is minus 2, a low likelihood (zero or less). Low-risk patients start with a D-dimer, which is sensitive: a negative result stops the work-up, and only a positive one leads to ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a first deep vein thrombosis of the left leg. Genetic testing shows factor V Leiden, an inherited clotting abnormality. Which component of the Virchow triad does this finding represent?","choices":["Venous insufficiency","Hypercoagulable state","Venous stasis","Endothelial trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is an inherited hypercoagulable state, listed with prothrombin gene mutation and protein C, protein S or antithrombin deficiency. In the Virchow triad it represents a clotting tendency of the blood, separate from stasis or vessel injury. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has long-standing pitting edema of the lower leg with brown pigmentation and a healed medial ankle ulcer. Duplex ultrasound shows thickened, scarred valve leaflets and chronic obstruction of the femoral vein. He does not recall any leg clot. Which is the most likely cause of his condition?","choices":["Congenital arteriovenous fistula","Pelvic vein neoplastic obstruction","Prior deep vein thrombosis","Progressive superficial reflux"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thickened, scarred valve leaflets with chronic obstruction of the femoral vein are the post-thrombotic picture, and prior deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is one of the most common causes of chronic venous insufficiency. Not recalling a clot does not exclude it: about 25% of these patients have no known history of DVT. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has visible, dilated, tortuous veins of the thigh and calf that are tender to palpation, with aching heaviness after prolonged standing. Which is the most common predisposing factor for her condition?","choices":["Recent pregnancy","Regular heavy lifting","Family history of varicose veins","Prolonged standing at work"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her dilated, tortuous, tender thigh and calf veins with aching heaviness after standing describe varicose veins. Among the risk factors, family history is named the most common predisposing factor for unwanted leg veins, ahead of prolonged standing, heavy lifting and pregnancy. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman had a left leg deep vein thrombosis 11 months ago. She now has chronic left leg heaviness, edema and brownish ankle skin that worsen with standing and improve with elevation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Recurrent deep vein thrombosis","Lymphedema","Drug-induced edema","Post-thrombotic syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Post-thrombotic (postphlebitic) syndrome is symptomatic chronic venous insufficiency that follows a deep vein thrombosis (DVT), from 6 to 24 months after the clot. Her left leg clot 11 months ago, followed by heaviness, edema and brownish ankle skin in that same leg, fits the window and the pattern. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer on active treatment presents to the emergency department with a swollen right leg. The entire leg is swollen and the right calf measures 4 cm more than the left. No alternative diagnosis is judged as likely as deep vein thrombosis, and no other Wells criteria are present. Which is the best next test?","choices":["Ankle-brachial index","Venous duplex ultrasound","D-dimer assay first","Contrast ascending venography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer on treatment 1 + entire leg swollen 1 + calf swelling over 3 cm 1 = 3, a high likelihood of deep vein thrombosis (DVT). A high-likelihood patient goes to ultrasound whatever a D-dimer shows, and duplex ultrasound has replaced venography. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on chemotherapy for breast cancer comes to the emergency department with a calf 4 cm larger than the other, tenderness along the deep veins and no better explanation, so her Wells score is high. Her D-dimer, drawn at triage, is negative. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Ordering a venous duplex ultrasound","Asking about shortness of breath","Measuring both calf circumferences","Ending the work-up at the D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer, calf swelling over 3 cm and deep venous tenderness give her a high Wells score. In high-risk patients the D-dimer is optional, and even a negative result leads on to ultrasound of the thigh, so stopping at the D-dimer would leave a likely deep vein thrombosis (DVT) unexamined. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a large arteriovenous fistula of the thigh and a positive Nicoladoni-Branham sign (slowing of the heart rate when the fistula is compressed). Her resting pulse is 88 beats per minute. Which action should the examiner avoid?","choices":["Feeling for a thrill over it","Checking pulses below it","Holding the fistula compressed","Listening over it for a bruit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her positive Nicoladoni-Branham sign means that compressing this large arteriovenous (AV) fistula reflexly slows her heart rate. Holding it compressed is the one action here that provokes that reflex, so sustained compression is what the examiner should avoid. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man hospitalized for six days with pneumonia develops swelling of the left calf. His team considers using the Wells criteria to estimate his probability of deep vein thrombosis. Which statement about applying the Wells criteria to him is accurate?","choices":["Meant for outpatient or emergency use","Most accurate in hospitalized patients","Designed for upper-extremity clots","A low score replaces all testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Wells criteria for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) are used only in the outpatient and emergency department settings, not for inpatients, and the score is less useful in hospitalized patients. He has been in hospital for six days, so the score does not fit his setting. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has been on strict bed rest for six days during an admission for pneumonia and now has swelling and aching of the entire left leg. Which component of the Virchow triad does his prolonged bed rest represent?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Arterial vasospasm","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Six days of strict bed rest is immobilization, which alters blood flow: the venous stasis arm of the Virchow triad. Immobilization carries about a 20-fold increased risk for deep vein thrombosis (DVT), which fits his new swelling and aching of the entire leg. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with a previous deep vein thrombosis two years ago comes to the emergency department with swelling of the entire right leg. She has no pitting edema, calf asymmetry over 3 cm, tenderness along the deep veins, collateral veins, cancer, recent bed rest, surgery or cast, and no better alternative diagnosis. By the Wells criteria, which likelihood category applies?","choices":["Low (score below 1)","High (score of 3 or more)","Moderate (score of 3 to 4)","Moderate (score of 1 to 2)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Previously documented deep vein thrombosis (DVT) 1 + entire leg swollen 1 = 2, with no other positive items and no subtraction for an alternative diagnosis. A score of 1 to 2 is a moderate likelihood; zero or less is low and 3 or more is high. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55 (Wells table pictures)"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a positive D-dimer during evaluation of calf pain two weeks after a bicycle crash with extensive bruising. He asks why his clinician says the result cannot confirm a clot. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It rises only with pulmonary embolism","It rises only with thigh clots","It reflects fibrinolysis from many causes","It is specific but not sensitive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An elevated D-dimer implies increased fibrinolysis, and the test is sensitive but not specific, so a positive result does not prove a deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Poor specificity means causes other than a clot, such as his recent crash with extensive bruising, can raise it; imaging is needed to confirm or exclude a clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a peripherally inserted central catheter for chemotherapy develops a tender cord in the upper arm. Venous duplex ultrasound shows extensive clot burden in a superficial vein. He has active cancer and a prior deep vein thrombosis. Which therapy should be added to compresses and an anti-inflammatory drug?","choices":["Aspirin alone","Anticoagulation","Systemic corticosteroids","Broad-spectrum antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His duplex ultrasound shows extensive clot burden, and active cancer with a prior deep vein thrombosis puts him at higher risk of thrombosis. In that setting anticoagulation may be added to warm compresses and an anti-inflammatory drug, using unfractionated heparin, enoxaparin (a low molecular weight heparin) or fondaparinux. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a painful ulcer above the medial malleolus with a clean base, fibrinous exudate and serous drainage, surrounded by brown pigmentation and pitting edema. Her pedal pulses are strong and her ankle-brachial index is 1.10. Which measure is essential for the ulcer to heal?","choices":["Topical antibiotic dressings alone","Oral flavonoid therapy alone","Early surgical valvuloplasty","Compression with edema control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A painful ulcer above the medial malleolus with brown pigmentation and pitting edema is a venous ulcer, and venous ulcers will not heal unless edema is controlled and compression is applied. Wound care referral supports healing, which can take 4 to 6 months. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has a congenital arteriovenous fistula of the calf with numerous small communications, causing chronic swelling and aching. She has no signs of ischemia or heart failure. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Ligation of every communication","Elastic support hose","Surgical excision of the fistula","Lifelong anticoagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A congenital arteriovenous (AV) fistula with numerous small communications is difficult to treat, so it is managed conservatively with elastic support hose. She has none of the complications to watch for, such as ischemia or heart failure, and embolization can sometimes remove a congenital fistula later. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a diagnosed leg deep vein thrombosis, not yet treated, develops sudden shortness of breath and pleuritic chest pain. Which complication has occurred?","choices":["Acute myocardial infarction","Pulmonary embolism","Post-thrombotic syndrome","Septic thrombophlebitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Part of a deep vein thrombus can break off and travel to the lungs, causing a pulmonary embolism. Her untreated leg clot followed by sudden shortness of breath and pleuritic chest pain fits that complication. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis of the forearm after an intravenous catheter is discharged on warm compresses, elevation and an anti-inflammatory drug. He is anxious that the clot could travel to his lungs. Which follow-up instruction is most appropriate?","choices":["Reexamination in 24 to 48 hours","Reexamination in 7 to 10 days","Reexamination in 6 to 8 weeks","Return only if fever develops"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His superficial thrombophlebitis is uncomplicated and already on the standard regimen of warm compresses, elevation and a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID). The next step is a repeat physical examination within 7 to 10 days to judge resolution versus progression; persistent or worsening signs then prompt a repeat duplex ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with chronic venous insufficiency and recurrent ulcers is being considered for an invasive venous procedure. Which test is the gold standard for defining his venous anatomy beforehand?","choices":["Catheter-based venography","Venous duplex ultrasound","Cross-sectional venography","Ankle-brachial index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He is about to have an invasive venous procedure, and catheter-based (invasive) venography is the gold standard test, done before an invasive intervention. It is rarely needed to make the diagnosis of chronic venous insufficiency, which duplex ultrasound handles first. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has dilated superficial leg veins with dull aching after standing. He has no edema, skin changes or ulceration, and his pedal pulses are normal. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Laser vein ablation","Vein sclerotherapy","Vein stripping surgery","Stockings and exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His varicose veins are isolated, with no edema, skin changes or ulceration, so management starts conservatively with leg elevation, exercise and compression stockings. His normal pedal pulses matter because arterial disease must be ruled out before compression, and stockings are tried for weeks to months before ablation is offered. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman must stay in bed for several days after major abdominal surgery. Her team wants a preventive measure aimed at the venous stasis component of the Virchow triad. Which measure is most appropriate?","choices":["Intermittent pneumatic compression","Inferior vena cava filter","Thrombolytic infusion","Warm compresses to the calves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She will be immobilized in bed after major surgery, which is exactly when intermittent pneumatic compression is used to prevent deep vein thrombosis (DVT). The cuffs inflate in sequence from the lower leg upward, moving blood out of the leg and countering venous stasis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 71"}]};
